    "body": "Kia announced that 2027 EV6 prices start at about $38,000, or $8,000 more than the new EV3.\n2027 Kia EV6 prices and driving range\nThe 2027 Kia EV6 arrives as a carryover model from last year. After reducing prices on the 2026 model year by $5,000 just a few months ago, the 2027 EV6 remains the same price across all trims.\nThe base EV6 Light standard-range RWD model starts at $37,900, not including the $1,545 destination fee, which is the same as last year.\nPowered by a 63 kWh battery, the standard-range variant offers up to 237 miles of range. Upgrading to the EV6 Light long range RWD model costs an extra $3,300, starting at $41,200, but delivers up to 319 miles of range using a larger 84 kWh battery.\nAdvertisement - scroll for more content\nThe 84 kWh battery offers up to 319 miles of range in RWD variants, while AWD EV6 models get up to 295 miles. The top-spec EV6 GT-Line AWD has up to 270 miles of range.\n2027 Kia EV6 (Source: Kia)\nKia said that with the EV6 MSRP unchanged and the launch of the new EV3, it’s “making EVs more attainable for those who embrace the fully electric lifestyle.”\nThe 2027 Kia EV3 starts at just $29,890, or about $8,000 less than the EV6. Kia’s entry-level electric SUV is available in five trims: Light, Wind, Land, GT-Line, and GT.\nThe lowest-priced EV3 Light FWD model uses a 58.3 kWh battery and delivers an EPA-estimated range of up to 221 miles.\n2027 Kia EV6 | Starting Price * | Driving Range\nEV6 Light SR RWD | $37,900 | 237 miles\nEV6 Light LR RWD | $41,200 | 319 miles\nEV6 Light LR AWD | $45,200 | 295 miles\nEV6 Wind RWD | $44,800 | 319 miles\nEV6 Wind AWD | $48,800 | 295 miles\nEV6 GT-Line RWD | $48,700 | 319 miles\nEV6 GT-Line AWD | $53,000 | 270 miles\n2027 Kia EV6 prices and driving range (*excluding $1,545 destination fee)\nHigher-priced trims, including the Wind, Land, GT-Line, and GT, use a larger 81.4 kWh battery that delivers up to 321 miles of range on a single charge. Like the EV6, the EV3 is also available with front-wheel-drive and all-wheel-drive powertrain options.\nInside, both the EV6 and EV3 feature Kia’s ccNC infotainment system. The setup includes nearly 30″ of screen space in a curved panoramic display, including dual 12.3″ instrument cluster and central navigation screens, plus a 5″ climate control touchscreen.\n2027 Kia EV6 interior (Source: Kia)\nBoth also include a native NACS port so that you can access Tesla Superchargers without a separate adapter.\nHowever, the EV6 uses an 800V architecture, enabling faster charging than the EV3, which uses a 400V system.\n2027 Kia EV3 (Source: Kia)\nThe EV6 can charge from 10% to 80% in about 18 minutes, whereas the EV3 takes about 30 minutes.\nThe EV6 is also larger and less boxy than the EV3, measuring 184.8″ long, 74″ wide, and 60.8″ tall, with a 114.2″ wheelbase. In comparison, the EV3 is 169.3″ long, 72.8″ wide, and 62″ tall, with a 105.5″ wheelbase.\nWhich one would you drive? The new EV3, the EV6, or would you go with Kia’s three-row EV9? Let us know in the comments.\nWith the new 2027 models arriving, Kia is offering a $10,000 customer cash discount on the 2026 EV9, $5,000 on the 2026 EV6, and $1,500 on the 2027 EV3 to clear inventory. You can use the links below to see what’s available near you.\nKia EV6 — See offers\nKia EV3 — See offers\nKia EV9 — See offers\nFTC: We use income earning auto affiliate links. More.\nStay up to date with the latest content by subscribing to Electrek on Google News . You’re reading Electrek— experts who break news about Tesla , electric vehicles, and green energy , day after day. Be sure to check out our homepage for all the latest news, and follow Electrek on X , Facebook , and LinkedIn to stay in the loop. Don’t know where to start? Check out our YouTube channel for the latest reviews.",
